{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:24:27Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 150, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "commit 7c7faaf0c57d8fe037ea150b39e8dbfe042b463b\nAuthor: ai-dev-switchboard <dev@ai-dev-switchboard.local>\nDate:   Mon Aug 17 12:09:05 2026 +0000\n\n    Round 5 retrospective: improve the pipeline's own instructions\n    \n    Changes proposed and applied by the retrospective role from round 5's logs. Scoped to agents/*.md and config/roles.d/*.role -- the instructions the pipeline carries, never the machinery. Reasoning in docs/agent-rework/round-05-retro.md.\n\ndiff --git a/agents/developer.md b/agents/developer.md\nindex a3a8b35..890a506 100644\n--- a/agents/developer.md\n+++ b/agents/developer.md\n@@ -48,6 +48,19 @@ editing the same file concurrently is how a run corrupts itself.\n    unvalidated trust boundaries. Fix what you introduce; do not leave it for\n    review to catch.\n \n+## When a tool call is denied\n+\n+If an Edit or Write is refused, **do not just report that you're blocked and\n+stop.** In a headless run there is no one to grant it, so retrying the same\n+call costs a turn and returns the same refusal \u2014 but giving up is worse: it\n+throws away the analysis you already did. Finish working out the change and\n+hand off the exact content \u2014 the file, the precise edit or new-file content,\n+and where it goes \u2014 so the next attempt can apply it directly instead of\n+re-reading the same spec and source files to re-derive a plan you already\n+had. State plainly which call was denied and why you could not apply it\n+yourself; that is a legitimate blocker. A one-line refusal that discards a\n+fully-worked-out change is not a complete handoff.\n+\n ## When work comes back to you\n \n Review and test findings come back with a must-fix list. Verify each finding", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01VdDu3LLxLiwcPdFejvMdnY", "session_id": "744b1940-c4ba-4635-afb0-ac675d200ec2"}}
