{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:24:38Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 164, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 4309 Aug 17 11:47 /home/dev/projects/ai-dev-switchboard/docs/agent-rework/round-06-retro.md\n---\n## Retrospective \u2014 Round 6\n\n**What happened:** Taiga #7 (\"show task counts\") landed on a branch (`feature/tp-7/...`) whose working tree already held complete, uncommitted work for a different ticket (Taiga #5, persistence) \u2014 a leftover from a prior cycle. `product-manager` found this immediately (`story-attempt1.output`), correctly diagnosed it as a workspace-hygiene problem per its own `agents/product-manager.md` instructions, and tried to execute exactly what those instructions prescribe: `git stash push -u` the foreign work onto the existing `feature/tp-5/...` branch. The sandbox denied that command twice (`story-attempt1.log:48-55`, `\"permission_denied\"` / `\"This command requires approval\"`), so it fell back to asking the user. Because `story` is a non-gate stage, the pipeline advanced anyway \u2014 `design`, `build` (both scopes, 3 attempts), and `review` (3 attempts) each independently re-ran `git status`/`git diff`/`git log`, reached the identical correct conclusion, and declined to fabricate a spec. The round consumed all 11 dispatches and blocked exactly as `max_attempts_per_stage=3` says it should (`summary.json`).\n\n**What cost time or quality:**\n- The actual blocker \u2014 `git stash` requiring approval with no human present in an unattended run (`story-attempt1.log:49,55`) \u2014 is a permission-profile gap in the target project's sandbox, not a prompt problem. `agents/product-manager.md`'s \"resolve it yourself\" instruction is correct but was unactionable here.\n- The real, fixable waste: `build-backend`/`build-frontend` attempts 2\u20133 and `review` attempts 2\u20133 each re-ran the full git investigation and rewrote a multi-paragraph diagnosis identical in substance to attempt 1 (compare `build-backend-attempt1.output`, `-attempt2.output`, `-attempt3.output`; same for `review-attempt{1,2,3}.output`), even though `pipeline_runner.py`'s `build_prompt` already hands every attempt \u22652 both the attempt number and the prior gate's full report. Six of the eleven dispatches added no new information.\n\n**What I changed:** Added a section to `agents/_conventions.md` (\"When a prior attempt already hit the same wall\") instructing any role that sees a repeat-attempt marker and a feedback block already diagnosing an out-of-role blocker to do one cheap confirmation check rather than the full investigation, then cite the prior report instead of restating it at length. This is a shared convention because the waste showed up identically in both `developer` (build) and `reviewer` dispatches, not one role's habit.\n\n**What I deliberately did not change:** `agents/product-manager.md`'s branch-untangling instructions \u2014 they're correct and only failed because of a denied permission, not because the instruction itself is wrong. `agents/reviewer.md`'s verdict vocabulary \u2014 I confirmed in `app/pipeline.py` that `blocked` and `changes-requested` are treated identically (`RETRY_VERDICTS`, both route to `on_fail=\"build\"`), so telling the reviewer to use `blocked` here would change nothing. No role fabricated work, gave conflicting answers, or overstepped scope this round \u2014 that discipline is working and isn't touched.\n\n**Needs a human:**\n1. The test-project's `.claude/settings.json` (or whatever permission profile governs unattended pipeline runs) denies `git stash`/similar mutating git commands outright, which makes `agents/product-manager.md`'s documented self-resolution path for exactly this scenario impossible to execute headlessly. Either grant scoped git-write permission for unattended `product-manager` dispatches, or accept that this class of workspace contamination will always need a manual approval step and adjust expectations accordingly.\n2. Machinery gap, not mine to fix: a gate's `on_fail` always targets `build` (`app/pipeline.py:91-95`), and non-gate stages always advance regardless of their own output (`Stage` class comment, `pipeline.py:67-69`). There's no route for \"this needs the orchestrator/product-manager, not another build attempt\" even though `review-attempt1/2/3` all said exactly that in prose. If this pattern recurs, the graph may need a distinct verdict/route for \"blocked upstream of this stage\" that skips straight to `story` or halts immediately instead of spending the full attempt budget on `build`\u2194`review`.\n\nVERDICT: done", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01WEWhTdbHr8TJeLf3KBuMVm", "session_id": "744b1940-c4ba-4635-afb0-ac675d200ec2"}}
